    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Immunotherapy using the adoptive transfer of selected cell populations is a promising approach to treat hematologic malignancies. Further, this approach may have merit in combination with hematopoietic stem cell transplant (HSCT), which is an established therapy, though with some limitations, such as cancer relapse and graft-versus-host disease (GVHD). Invariant natural killer T (iNKT) cells are a subset of innate lymphocytes with antitumor effects and immunoregulatory properties yet without GVHD induction potential. Preclinical studies have demonstrated that iNKT cell expansion and proliferation upon adoptive transfer requires cytokine support through signaling induced by cytokines of the gamma-chain family, particularly interleukin-2 (IL-2). In order to investigate the antitumor potential of murine iNKT cells, iNKT cells expanded ex vivo with IL-2 and IL-15 were cultured with a murine lymphoma cell line in vitro as well as tested in a lymphoma tumor model in vivo. While cytotoxicity was observed in vitro, the effects of the iNKT alone were not sufficient to rescue the mice in vivo. Therefore, to potentially enhance the biological activity of iNKT cells, we explored a cytokine-cytokine receptor complex to transduce onto the iNKT cells. The complex consisted of an engineered orthogonal IL-2 receptor-β (ortho-IL-2Rβ) that pairs with an orthogonal IL-2 (ortho-IL-2), which preferentially expands the transduced effector immune cells while avoiding toxicities associated with IL-2 administration. The successful generation of this product establishes the opportunity to investigate the effects of the engineered IL-2 cytokine-cytokine receptor complex on the cytotoxic capacity of iNKT cells. Collectively, these results demonstrate the great potential of iNKT cells to act as an antitumor adoptive transfer therapy.",
